<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicholas I Mystikos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">852–925</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Nicholas Mystikos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicholas I Mystikos was Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> from 901 to 907 and again from 912 until his death. He was deposed for opposing the fourth marriage of Emperor Leo VI, then restored, and led the regency for the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> in 913–914, when he crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-i-of-bulgaria">Simeon I of Bulgaria</a>. His letters record the mission that converted the Alans to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> around 916.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicholas-i-mystikos.jpg" alt="Nicholas I Mystikos" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nikolai Pavlovic (1835-1894) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicholas_Mystikos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
